{"`isConditionalPasskeysEnabled()` is called without the required `UserModel` parameter. The only definition in `UsernamePasswordForm` is `isConditionalPasskeysEnabled(UserModel user)`, so this will fail to compile. It should be `isConditionalPasskeysEnabled(context.getUser())`.": 0.3641725182533264, "When `context.getUser()` is null (initial login page), the short-circuit evaluation skips the passkey check entirely. The parent's `authenticate()` also won't call `webauthnAuth.fillContextForm(context)` because `isConditionalPasskeysEnabled(null)` returns false. This means the conditional passkey UI won't be shown on the initial login form, even though discoverable passkeys don't require a known user. Consider checking passkey enablement independently of the user null check so the UI is rendered on first visit.": 0.5783965587615967, "Potential `NullPointerException`: the `user` variable used inside this block (passed to `WebAuthnAuthenticatorsBean`) is no longer guarded by a direct null check. Since `shouldShowWebAuthnAuthenticators` is `protected` and designed to be overridden, a subclass could return `true` while `context.getUser()` is null. Consider adding a null guard on `user` inside the block, or documenting the null-safety contract in the Javadoc.": 0.2101878523826599, "Missing `@Override` annotation on `shouldShowWebAuthnAuthenticators` method in `WebAuthnConditionalUIAuthenticator`. This method overrides `WebAuthnAuthenticator.shouldShowWebAuthnAuthenticators` \u2014 adding the annotation ensures a compile-time error if the parent signature changes.": 0.7948797345161438, "The first webauthn login event is never asserted in the test, unlike the sibling tests `webauthnLoginWithExternalKey_reauthentication` and `passwordLoginWithExternalKey`. If the initial authentication produces an unexpected event, this test won't catch it. Consider adding `events.expectLogin()...assertEvent()` after the first `appPage.assertCurrent()`.": 0.44746431708335876, "Catching `Exception` is too broad and the `assertThat`/`fail` lines are unreachable. `findElement` either returns a non-null element (never null) or throws `NoSuchElementException`. If an unexpected exception type is thrown (e.g. `StaleElementReferenceException`), this catch will silently swallow it, making the test pass incorrectly. Use a targeted catch instead.": 0.7737441658973694}